WEBVTT

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:23.280
I never knew the old Vienna before the war with its Strauss music, its glamour and easy charm.

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:25.460
Constantinople suited me better.

00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:29.540
I really got to know it in the classic period of the black market.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.880
run anything if people wanted it enough and had the money to pay. Of course, a situation

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:38.840
like that does tempt amateurs, but, well, they, you know, they can't stay the course

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:43.470
like a professional. Now, the city, it's divided into four zones, you know, each occupied

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:47.680
by a power, the American, the British, the Russian, and the French. But the center of

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:51.520
the city, that's international police by an international patrol, one member of each of

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:56.310
the four powers. Wonderful. What a hope they had, all strangers to the place, and none

00:02:56.310 --> 00:03:00.000
of them could speak the same language, except a sort of smattering of Germans.

00:03:00.990 --> 00:03:03.390
Good fellows on the whole did their best, you know.

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:07.180
Vienna doesn't really look any worse than a lot of other European cities.

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:08.560
Bombed about a bit.

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:11.860
Oh, I was gonna tell you, wait, I was gonna tell you about Harley Martins, an American.

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:13.960
Came all the way here to visit a friend of his.

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:16.370
The name was Lime, Harry Lime.

00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:19.970
Now, Martins was broke and Lime had offered him some sort of, I don't know, some sort of a job.

00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:21.950
Anyway, there he was, poor chap.

00:03:22.030 --> 00:03:25.090
Happy as a lark and without a cent.

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:37.640
Come forward, please.

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:40.640
- What's the purpose of your visit here? - A friend offered me a job here.

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:43.470
- Where are you staying? - With him, 15 Stiff Gossips.

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:45.420
- His name? - Lime, Harry Lime.

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:48.600
- Okay. - I thought he'd be here to meet me.

00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:23.340
You're not lucky, my dear sir. You're ten minutes too late.

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:26.400
No one's here anymore. The people are free.

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:30.000
- Speak English? - English?

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:34.290
Little, little.

00:04:34.290 --> 00:04:40.050
10 minutes too late.

00:04:40.050 --> 00:04:41.360
Already gone.

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:42.610
Who?

00:04:42.610 --> 00:04:48.190
His friends and the coffin.

00:04:48.190 --> 00:04:49.010
Coven?

00:04:49.010 --> 00:04:50.090
Mr. Limes.

00:04:50.090 --> 00:04:55.970
An accident knocked over by a car here in front of the house.

00:04:55.970 --> 00:04:57.230
Have seen it myself.

00:04:57.230 --> 00:04:59.130
Killed at once immediately.

00:04:59.130 --> 00:05:00.000
Already in--

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:09.000
in heaven. Sorry for the grave diggers. Hard work. It is frost.

00:05:10.010 --> 00:05:25.510
Blime.

00:05:35.850 --> 00:05:38.930
...of Pontius Pilate, crucified, stoned and buried...

00:05:39.050 --> 00:05:42.680
...and ascended to hell, rose again on the third day from the dead...

00:05:42.730 --> 00:05:46.490
...and ascended to heaven, seated on the right hand of God Almighty...

00:05:46.610 --> 00:05:49.750
- Who is He? - ...and to whom we come to judge the living and the dead.

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:53.080
I believe in the Holy Spirit, the Holy Catholic Church...

00:05:53.110 --> 00:05:55.210
...the communion of the saints, the remission of sins...

00:05:55.330 --> 00:05:58.200
...the resurrection of flesh and eternal life. Amen.

00:05:58.490 --> 00:06:00.000
Lord, give him eternal rest.

00:06:00.190 --> 00:06:03.880
May he rest in peace.

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:11.140
In the name of the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit. Amen.

00:06:54.380 --> 00:06:58.130
Like a lift to town? I've got a car here.

00:07:02.010 --> 00:07:21.800
My name's Calloway. Friend alive. Been here long?

00:07:33.390 --> 00:07:37.240
You've been in a bit of a shock, haven't you? You could do with a drink.

00:07:37.340 --> 00:07:39.760
If you buy me one, I haven't got any Austrian clothes. Thanks.

00:07:39.880 --> 00:07:41.010
Schmolker?

00:07:41.570 --> 00:07:45.940
I guess nobody knew Harry like he did, or like I did.

00:07:46.590 --> 00:07:49.940
- How long ago? - Back in school.

00:07:50.920 --> 00:07:53.430
He was never so lonesome in my life till he showed up.

00:07:53.790 --> 00:07:57.110
- When did you see him last? - September 39th.

00:07:57.270 --> 00:07:58.790
- When the business started? - Uh-uh.

00:07:58.890 --> 00:08:00.000
See much of him before that?

00:08:00.030 --> 00:08:01.760
Once in a while.

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:03.090
Best friend I ever had.

00:08:03.090 --> 00:08:06.500
That sounds like a cheap novelette.

00:08:06.500 --> 00:08:10.430
Why write cheap novelette?

00:08:10.430 --> 00:08:11.730
I'm afraid I've never heard of you.

00:08:11.730 --> 00:08:13.370
What's your name again?

00:08:13.370 --> 00:08:15.250
Holly Martin.

00:08:15.250 --> 00:08:16.000
Sorry.

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:19.330
You ever hear of the Lone Rider of Santa Fe?

00:08:19.330 --> 00:08:21.220
Can't say that I have.

00:08:21.220 --> 00:08:22.820
Death at Double X Ranch?

00:08:22.820 --> 00:08:24.760
Uh, Raunch?

00:08:24.760 --> 00:08:26.770
Nope.

00:08:27.640 --> 00:08:29.150
You must have known I was broke.

00:08:29.150 --> 00:08:30.000
You even sent me a...

00:08:30.120 --> 00:08:32.900
Now, plane ticket is a shame.

00:08:33.270 --> 00:08:36.310
- What? - Him dying like that.

00:08:37.650 --> 00:08:39.000
Best thing that ever happened to him.

00:08:40.770 --> 00:08:41.970
What are you trying to say?

00:08:42.170 --> 00:08:45.370
He was about the worst racketeer that ever made a dirty living in this city.

00:08:45.570 --> 00:08:48.280
- Policeman, huh? - Come on. Have another drink.

00:08:49.050 --> 00:08:51.820
I never did like policemen. I have to call him sheriff.

00:08:52.020 --> 00:08:55.090
- Ever seen one? - Pin it on a dead man.

00:08:55.290 --> 00:08:59.540
Some petty racket with gasoline or something.

00:09:00.350 --> 00:09:01.770
- Just like I'm a cop.

00:09:01.770 --> 00:09:03.610
- You're a real cop, I suppose.

00:09:03.610 --> 00:09:04.610
- It wasn't petrol.

00:09:04.610 --> 00:09:06.950
- So it wasn't petrol.

00:09:06.950 --> 00:09:10.140
So it was tires or saccharine.

00:09:10.140 --> 00:09:12.900
Why don't you catch a few murderers for a change?

00:09:12.900 --> 00:09:18.230
- Well, you could say that murder was part of his racket.

00:09:18.230 --> 00:09:19.420
It's all right, Payne.

00:09:19.420 --> 00:09:22.030
He's only a scribbler with too much drink in him.

00:09:22.030 --> 00:09:24.930
Take Mr. Holly Martin's home.

00:09:24.930 --> 00:09:27.030
- Holly Martin, sir?

00:09:27.030 --> 00:09:30.000
The, er, the writer, the author of

00:09:30.030 --> 00:09:31.110
of death at XXX, right?

00:09:31.110 --> 00:09:33.750
- Listen, Callaghan. - Callaway, I'm English, not Irish.

00:09:33.750 --> 00:09:36.570
You're not going to close your files at a dead man's expense.

00:09:36.570 --> 00:09:38.700
So you're going to find me the real criminal.

00:09:38.700 --> 00:09:40.180
Sounds like one of your stories.

00:09:40.180 --> 00:09:43.340
When I'm finished with you, you'll leave Vienna, you'll look so silly.

00:09:43.340 --> 00:09:44.560
Here's some army money.

00:09:44.560 --> 00:09:46.520
Should see you through tonight at Sarkis Hotel

00:09:46.520 --> 00:09:48.480
if you don't drink too much in the bar.

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:55.160
We'll keep a seat for you on tomorrow's plane.

00:09:55.160 --> 00:09:57.480
Please be careful, sir.

00:09:57.480 --> 00:09:58.870
Up we come.

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:03.980
- Have you ever written anything lately? - Take him to Sarker's. Don't hit him again if he behaves.

00:10:04.070 --> 00:10:07.230
And you go carefully there. It's a military hotel.

00:10:07.300 --> 00:10:10.440
I'm so glad to have met you, sir. I've read quite a few of your books.

00:10:10.500 --> 00:10:14.280
- Neither do we have. - I like a good western.

00:10:14.370 --> 00:10:18.220
That's what I like about them, sir. You can pick them up and put them down any time.

00:10:18.280 --> 00:10:19.500
- Now, Mr. Hartmut. - Yes, sir.

00:10:19.600 --> 00:10:21.800
Major Calloway said this gentleman's got to have a room for the night.

00:10:21.900 --> 00:10:24.360
- He'll be off tomorrow. - Last word, please.

00:10:24.510 --> 00:10:30.000
I'd better want to introduce you to everybody in English.

00:10:31.760 --> 00:10:33.300
- Mr. Crabbin. - What is it, Sergeant?

00:10:33.380 --> 00:10:34.760
- Mr. Holly Martins, sir. - Who?

00:10:34.840 --> 00:10:37.540
- The author. I thought you might be interested. - Never heard of him.

00:10:37.620 --> 00:10:40.880
- He's very good, sir. I've read quite a few of his books. - Have you, Sergeant?

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:43.480
- Martins. - Thank you, Sergeant.

00:10:43.480 --> 00:10:49.920
Mr. Martins. My name's Crabbin. I represent the CRS of GHQ.

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:53.260
- You do? - Yes, cultural re-education section.

00:10:53.340 --> 00:10:55.710
Propaganda, very important in a place like this.

00:10:55.790 --> 00:10:58.480
We do a little show each week. Last week, we had Hamlet.

00:10:58.560 --> 00:11:00.000
The week before, we had, um...

00:11:00.830 --> 00:11:02.990
- The striptease, sir? - Yes, the Hindu dancers.

00:11:03.070 --> 00:11:04.150
Thank you, Sergeant.

00:11:04.230 --> 00:11:07.150
This is the first opportunity we've had of making an American author welcome.

00:11:07.230 --> 00:11:08.930
- Welcome? - Now, tell you what, Mr. Martins.

00:11:09.010 --> 00:11:11.930
On Wednesday night at our institute, we're having a little lecture...

00:11:12.010 --> 00:11:14.850
on the contemporary novel. Thought perhaps you'd like to speak.

00:11:14.930 --> 00:11:16.710
- They wouldn't know me. - Oh, nonsense.

00:11:16.800 --> 00:11:19.190
Your novels are very popular here, aren't they, Sergeant?

00:11:19.270 --> 00:11:21.190
- Very popular, sir. - Very popular.

00:11:21.270 --> 00:11:25.670
- Are you staying long? - How long can Wednesday here on this stage money?

00:11:25.750 --> 00:11:30.000
Listen, Mr. Martins, if you'd agree to be our guest, we'd be delighted to have you.

00:11:32.720 --> 00:11:34.480
- Would you? - As long as you care to stay.

00:11:34.560 --> 00:11:38.000
- But it's due to leave tomorrow, sir. - Excuse me, have you got toothache?

00:11:38.080 --> 00:11:40.580
- Number eight, Mr. Mullins. - Come upstairs a moment, dear.

00:11:40.660 --> 00:11:43.700
- I know a very good dentist. - I don't need a daddy. Somebody hit me, that's all.

00:11:43.780 --> 00:11:46.620
- Did he? - Goodness, you just brought that to the police. Were they trying to rob you?

00:11:46.700 --> 00:11:51.160
- I'm just a soldier. I was trying to punch his major in the eye. - No, a major, were you really?

00:11:51.320 --> 00:11:54.390
- Heard of Harry Lyme? - Well, I've heard of him, of course, but I didn't exactly know him.

00:11:54.470 --> 00:11:58.040
- Well, what's going to stay with him, but he died, thirsty? - Goodness, that's awkward.

00:11:58.130 --> 00:11:59.890
That's what you say to people after death?

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:02.950
- That's all good. - Mr. Martins. Excuse me.

00:12:02.950 --> 00:12:05.490
- Telephone. - Who is it?

00:12:05.490 --> 00:12:09.670
- Baron Kurz. - It's a mistake, yes?

00:12:09.670 --> 00:12:11.590
I was a friend of Harry Lyme.

00:12:11.590 --> 00:12:14.700
I'd very much like to meet you, Baron. Come around.

00:12:14.700 --> 00:12:17.830
Austrians aren't allowed in your hotel.

00:12:17.830 --> 00:12:20.810
- Couldn't we meet at the Mozart Cafe? - Where?

00:12:20.810 --> 00:12:24.300
- Just around the corner. - How'll I know you?

00:12:24.300 --> 00:12:27.570
I'll carry a copy of one of your books. Harry gave it to me.

00:12:27.570 --> 00:12:29.890
Be there in a moment.

00:12:30.410 --> 00:12:33.490
- Wait a minute. - If I do this lecture business, you'll put me up here a while?

00:12:33.490 --> 00:12:35.060
- Certainly. - It's a deal.

00:12:35.060 --> 00:12:37.910
You ever read a book of mine called The Lone Rider of Santa Fe?

00:12:37.910 --> 00:12:38.910
No, not that one, sir.

00:12:38.910 --> 00:12:42.170
It's a story about a man who hunted down a sheriff who was victimizing his best friend.

00:12:42.170 --> 00:12:43.800
- Seems exciting. - It is.

00:12:43.800 --> 00:12:47.520
I'm gunning just the same way for you, Major Callahan.

00:12:47.520 --> 00:12:50.520
Sounds really pretty, sir.

00:13:03.320 --> 00:13:05.060
- Kurtz? - Mr. Martins?

00:13:05.610 --> 00:13:08.640
Delighted to meet you. Come, let's sit down here.

00:13:08.900 --> 00:13:13.140
- Over. - Zwei. What do you like? Tea? Coffee?

00:13:13.220 --> 00:13:16.200
- Coffee. - Ma schwarze, ma schwarze.

00:13:16.280 --> 00:13:20.170
- It's wonderful how you keep the tension. - Tension?

00:13:20.250 --> 00:13:22.090
- Suspense. - You really liked it?

00:13:22.170 --> 00:13:25.390
At the end of every chapter, you are left guessing what he'll be up to next.

00:13:25.470 --> 00:13:30.000
- So, you were a friend of Harry's? - I think he's best. Except you.

00:13:31.400 --> 00:13:35.240
The police have a crazy notion that he was mixed up in some sort of a racket.

00:13:36.070 --> 00:13:37.720
Everyone in Vienna is.

00:13:38.360 --> 00:13:40.920
We all sell cigarettes and that kind of thing.

00:13:41.810 --> 00:13:46.550
I tell you, I've done things that would have seemed unthinkable before the war.

00:13:47.290 --> 00:13:51.210
Once, when I was hard on, I sold some tires on the black market.

00:13:52.210 --> 00:13:54.360
I wonder what my father said.

00:13:54.480 --> 00:13:56.500
I'm afraid the police meant more than that.

00:13:56.580 --> 00:13:59.540
They get rather absurd ideas sometimes.

00:14:00.570 --> 00:14:04.960
he's somewhere now he won't mind about that even so i'm not going to leave it at this

00:14:04.960 --> 00:14:16.010
will you help me i wish i could but you know i'm an austrian i have to be careful with the police

00:14:17.300 --> 00:14:30.000
i'm afraid i can't help you except with advice of course advice we came out of his place like this and we are both

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:39.310
This way a friend of his called to him from over there Harry went across and from up there came the truck

00:14:39.310 --> 00:14:53.900
It was just about here here. Yes, his friend and I picked him up carried him across over here

00:14:55.000 --> 00:14:57.170
It was a terrible thing

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:07.280
We laid him down just about here, and this is where he died.

00:15:07.700 --> 00:15:10.150
Even at the end, his thoughts were of you.

00:15:10.150 --> 00:15:11.180
What did he say?

00:15:11.180 --> 00:15:14.430
I can't remember the exact words, Holly.

00:15:14.430 --> 00:15:16.520
I may call you Holly, mayn't I?

00:15:16.520 --> 00:15:18.430
He always called you that to us.

00:15:18.430 --> 00:15:21.710
He was anxious I should look after you when you arrived,

00:15:21.710 --> 00:15:25.670
to see that you got safely home, and tickets, you know, and all that.

00:15:25.670 --> 00:15:27.620
But he said he died instantaneously.

00:15:31.310 --> 00:15:38.610
- Well, he died before the ambulance could reach us. - Well, it was only you and this, uh, this friend of his, uh, who is he?

00:15:38.610 --> 00:15:42.090
- A Romanian. Mr. Popesco. - I'd like to talk to him.

00:15:42.090 --> 00:15:50.960
- He has left Vienna. - Excuse me.

00:15:50.960 --> 00:15:54.880
- Yes? - Did you know Mr. Lime well?

00:15:54.880 --> 00:15:56.560
- Mr. Lime? - Yes.

00:15:56.560 --> 00:15:59.380
- Oh, you remember me up upstairs. - Yes, I remember you.

00:15:59.380 --> 00:16:00.000
Well, who used to be?

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:02.430
You used to visit Mr. Lime.

00:16:02.430 --> 00:16:03.660
Visit?

00:16:03.660 --> 00:16:04.890
Was will er wissen?

00:16:04.890 --> 00:16:07.000
We will wissen, wer hier verkehrt hat.

00:16:07.000 --> 00:16:07.910
Ach so.

00:16:07.910 --> 00:16:09.920
Es kommen so viele Leute her.

00:16:09.920 --> 00:16:13.260
Sie waren da, der Obescu und...

00:16:13.260 --> 00:16:15.120
Ich kann nicht alle kennen.

00:16:15.120 --> 00:16:16.070
What would he say?

00:16:16.070 --> 00:16:18.070
He says he doesn't know everybody.

00:16:18.070 --> 00:16:20.470
Karl, kannst du einen Moment hereinkommen?

00:16:20.470 --> 00:16:22.810
Moment, ja.

00:16:22.810 --> 00:16:23.630
Schauen Sie, ich bin...

00:16:23.630 --> 00:16:25.820
Du musst ans Telefon.

00:16:25.820 --> 00:16:27.970
Excuse me.

00:16:31.660 --> 00:16:35.360
- Who's at the funeral beside you? - Only his doctor, Dr. Winkel.

00:16:35.360 --> 00:16:40.090
- Wasn't there a girl there? - Some girl of the Josefstadt Theater.

00:16:40.090 --> 00:16:45.710
You know what Harry was. You oughtn't to speak to her. It would only cause her pain.

00:16:45.710 --> 00:16:51.760
- Not necessarily. She probably would a hell. - What's the good of another post-mortem?

00:16:51.760 --> 00:16:55.690
Suppose you dig up something, well, discreditable to Harry.

00:16:55.690 --> 00:17:00.000
- Would you give me your address? - I live in the Russian sector, but you

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:02.590
You'll find me at the Cousin Over Club every night.

00:17:02.590 --> 00:17:04.870
One has to work the best way one can, you know.

00:17:04.870 --> 00:17:06.410
- What's the name of this girl?

00:17:06.410 --> 00:17:08.270
- I don't know.

00:17:08.270 --> 00:17:09.820
I don't think I ever heard it.

00:17:09.820 --> 00:17:12.780
- You did mention the theater.

00:17:12.780 --> 00:17:14.970
- Josefstadt.

00:17:14.970 --> 00:17:19.710
But I still think it won't have any good.

00:17:19.710 --> 00:17:22.780
You do better to think of yourself.

00:17:22.780 --> 00:17:24.650
- I'll be all right.

00:17:24.650 --> 00:17:25.570
- Of course.

00:17:25.570 --> 00:17:27.710
- I'm so glad I've met you.

00:17:27.710 --> 00:17:30.000
- A master of suspense.

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:34.900
Such a good cover, I think.

00:17:34.900 --> 00:17:35.820
Number eight, please.

00:17:35.820 --> 00:17:36.820
Major Canaway's compliment, sir.

00:17:36.820 --> 00:17:38.820
And here's the ticket for the plane tomorrow.

00:17:38.820 --> 00:17:40.970
You tell the Major I won't need it.

00:17:40.970 --> 00:17:43.920
Oh, Porter, order me a ticket tonight for the Josephstadt Theatre.

00:17:43.920 --> 00:17:45.890
Ah, Mr. Martins, good evening.

00:17:45.890 --> 00:17:47.890
Sir, I was to drive you to the air fuel to take you to the bus, sir.

00:17:47.890 --> 00:17:48.890
Whichever you prefer.

00:17:48.890 --> 00:17:52.900
Didn't you hear Mr. Craven offered me the hospitality of the HQ BMT?

00:17:52.900 --> 00:17:54.900
Ich bin dich.

00:17:54.900 --> 00:17:55.900
Ich bin dich.

00:17:55.900 --> 00:17:57.900
Ich bin dich.

00:17:57.900 --> 00:18:00.000
Und du musst jetzt nur nicht streng zu kommen.

00:18:01.950 --> 00:18:05.790
Oh, for God's sake, not just that. My dear children.

00:18:05.910 --> 00:18:08.630
If I don't tell your mother that you always poke her in the cheek,

00:18:08.750 --> 00:18:10.090
will you let me marry Wolfgang?

00:18:10.210 --> 00:18:11.590
That's a blunder.

00:18:11.710 --> 00:18:14.210
Really?

00:18:14.330 --> 00:18:18.600
If I didn't have the three girls, I could have given him the three crosses.

00:18:18.720 --> 00:18:21.250
They can't be more poisonous.

00:18:46.760 --> 00:19:00.000
was a friend of harry lime

00:19:00.600 --> 00:19:18.740
Come in.

00:19:18.740 --> 00:19:20.200
Thank you.

00:19:20.200 --> 00:19:21.250
Sit down.

00:19:21.250 --> 00:19:22.140
Thank you.

00:19:22.140 --> 00:19:23.140
I enjoy the play very much.

00:19:23.140 --> 00:19:24.130
Excuse me.

00:19:24.130 --> 00:19:25.130
Good enough, Anna.

00:19:25.130 --> 00:19:26.130
Good enough.

00:19:26.130 --> 00:19:27.140
You were awfully good.

00:19:27.140 --> 00:19:29.140
Do you understand German?

00:19:29.140 --> 00:19:30.000
No, no.

00:19:30.570 --> 00:19:32.920
Excuse me. I could follow it fine.

00:19:32.920 --> 00:19:36.520
Perhaps Harry told you about me. My name is Holly Martins.

00:19:36.520 --> 00:19:38.820
No, he never told me about his friends.

00:19:38.820 --> 00:19:39.820
Would you like some tea?

00:19:39.820 --> 00:19:40.820
Thank you.

00:19:40.820 --> 00:19:42.780
Someone threw me this packet last week.

00:19:42.780 --> 00:19:45.750
Sometimes the British do instead of flowers, you know, on the first night.

00:19:45.750 --> 00:19:49.190
That was a bouquet, too, from an American. Would you rather have whiskey?

00:19:49.190 --> 00:19:50.330
Tea's fine.

00:19:50.330 --> 00:19:52.440
Good. I wanted to sell it.

00:19:52.440 --> 00:19:55.310
Oh, there's some tea left.

00:19:55.310 --> 00:19:57.260
Have you known him some time?

00:19:57.260 --> 00:20:00.000
Yes.

00:20:00.600 --> 00:20:02.600
I wanted to talk to you. No, thank you.

00:20:02.600 --> 00:20:04.230
I wanted to talk to you about him.

00:20:04.230 --> 00:20:06.690
There's nothing really to talk about, is there?

00:20:06.690 --> 00:20:08.490
Nothing.

00:20:08.490 --> 00:20:11.560
Well, I saw you at the funeral.

00:20:11.560 --> 00:20:13.930
I'm so sorry. I didn't notice much.

00:20:13.930 --> 00:20:19.320
You were in love with him, weren't you?

00:20:19.320 --> 00:20:21.240
I don't know.

00:20:21.240 --> 00:20:23.360
How can you know a thing like that afterwards?

00:20:23.360 --> 00:20:26.030
I don't know anything anymore except

00:20:26.030 --> 00:20:27.860
I want to be dead too.

00:20:27.860 --> 00:20:29.480
Some more tea?

00:20:30.640 --> 00:20:35.310
- No, tea, like a cigarette.

00:20:35.310 --> 00:20:37.330
- Oh, American, thank you, you like them.

00:20:37.330 --> 00:20:39.930
- I was talking to another friend of Harry's,

00:20:39.930 --> 00:20:42.980
Baron Kurtz, you know him?

00:20:42.980 --> 00:20:44.220
- No.

00:20:44.220 --> 00:20:45.590
- He's got a little dog.

00:20:45.590 --> 00:20:47.410
- Oh yes, yes.

00:20:47.410 --> 00:20:49.810
- Don't understand what Harry saw in a fellow like that.

00:20:49.810 --> 00:20:51.390
- That was the man who brought me some money

00:20:51.390 --> 00:20:53.190
when Harry died.

00:20:53.190 --> 00:20:55.570
He said Harry had been anxious at the last moment.

00:20:55.570 --> 00:21:00.000
- Oh, he said he remembered me too, seems to show

00:21:00.670 --> 00:21:02.190
- Wasn't in much pain.

00:21:02.190 --> 00:21:04.480
- Dr. Winkle told me that.

00:21:04.480 --> 00:21:07.480
- Dr. Winkle, who's he?

00:21:07.480 --> 00:21:09.280
- A doctor Harry used to go to.

00:21:09.280 --> 00:21:12.030
He was passing just after it happened.

00:21:12.030 --> 00:21:12.950
- His own doctor?

00:21:12.950 --> 00:21:14.990
- Yes.

00:21:14.990 --> 00:21:18.090
- Well, were you at the inquest?

00:21:18.090 --> 00:21:20.590
- Yes, they said it wasn't the driver's fault.

00:21:20.590 --> 00:21:23.870
Harry had often said what a careful driver he was.

00:21:23.870 --> 00:21:25.390
- He was Harry's driver?

00:21:25.390 --> 00:21:27.020
- Uh-huh.

00:21:27.020 --> 00:21:28.890
- Well, I...

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:38.080
get it. All of them there. Kurtz, this Romanian Popesco, his own driver knocking him over,

00:21:38.180 --> 00:21:44.490
his own doctor just passing by. No strangers there at all. I know. I've wondered about

00:21:44.490 --> 00:21:55.250
it a hundred times if it really was an accident. What difference does it make? He's dead, isn't

00:21:55.250 --> 00:21:59.800
he? But if it wasn't a... Yeah, Frau Huber. Please be great.

00:22:00.700 --> 00:22:01.580
Yeah, I guess, Sean.

00:22:01.930 --> 00:22:02.720
I must hurry.

00:22:02.860 --> 00:22:03.980
They don't like us to use the lights.

00:22:04.760 --> 00:22:05.700
Porter saw it happen.

00:22:05.980 --> 00:22:06.940
Then why worry?

00:22:08.020 --> 00:22:11.910
Do you know that, Porter?

00:22:15.080 --> 00:22:15.520
Yes.

00:22:17.410 --> 00:22:18.290
It's right there.

00:22:18.980 --> 00:22:20.290
There is what happened.

00:22:20.800 --> 00:22:21.950
Eh, it happened.

00:22:22.190 --> 00:22:22.970
English happened.

00:22:23.010 --> 00:22:23.750
What's he saying?

00:22:23.990 --> 00:22:25.470
He says it happened right down there.

00:22:25.550 --> 00:22:26.170
Happened, yes.

00:22:26.310 --> 00:22:27.390
Happened right down there.

00:22:28.030 --> 00:22:28.730
You saw it?

00:22:29.220 --> 00:22:30.000
Well, not so.

00:22:31.120 --> 00:22:43.980
I heard the brakes, and I got to the window and saw them carry the body to the other side of the Josef, the Josef, Emperor Josef statue.

00:22:43.980 --> 00:22:46.070
Why didn't they bring him in the house?

00:23:11.580 --> 00:23:13.080
Could he have been conscious?

00:23:13.080 --> 00:23:14.200
- Conscious.

00:23:14.200 --> 00:23:18.800
- Was he, was he, was he still alive?

00:23:18.800 --> 00:23:22.440
- Alive, he couldn't have been alive.

00:23:22.440 --> 00:23:25.560
Not with his head, in the way it was.

00:23:25.560 --> 00:23:27.870
- I was told that he did not die at once.

00:23:27.870 --> 00:23:30.000
-

00:23:30.060 --> 00:23:32.500
- No, I mean... - "Er war gleich tot."

00:23:32.500 --> 00:23:34.300
- Willen Sie? "Gleich tot." Moment. - No, you...

00:23:34.380 --> 00:23:35.600
Wait a bissel, moment.

00:23:35.600 --> 00:23:40.540
"Fran Schmidt, bitte, wie sagt man auf Englisch, er war gleich tot."

00:23:40.560 --> 00:23:44.380
- He was quite dead. - He was... Ah, ja, he was quite dead, yes.

00:23:44.460 --> 00:23:46.290
He was quite dead.

00:23:46.290 --> 00:23:48.230
But this sounds crazy.

00:23:48.240 --> 00:23:52.550
If he was killed at once, how could he have talked about me and this lady here after he was dead?

00:23:52.600 --> 00:23:54.770
Why didn't you say all this at the inquest?

00:23:54.770 --> 00:23:59.790
Eh, it's better not to be mixed up in things like this.

00:24:00.030 --> 00:24:07.120
- Things like what? - I was not the only one who did not give evidence.

00:24:07.200 --> 00:24:11.840
- Who else? - Three men helped to carry your friend to the statue.

00:24:11.910 --> 00:24:17.980
- Kurtz, the Romanian, and... - There was a third man. He didn't give evidence.

00:24:18.060 --> 00:24:23.960
- You don't mean the doctor? - No, no, no. He came later, after they carried him to the Josef statue.

00:24:24.040 --> 00:24:28.360
- What did this man look like? - I didn't see his face.

00:24:31.310 --> 00:24:37.580
He didn't look up. He was quite gewöhnlich, ordinary.

00:24:37.580 --> 00:24:46.450
He might have been just anybody. Just anybody.

00:24:46.450 --> 00:24:52.760
Hello? Hello?

00:24:52.760 --> 00:24:53.780
Who is that?

00:24:53.780 --> 00:24:55.990
Hello.

00:24:55.990 --> 00:24:59.820
Why don't you answer?

00:25:06.040 --> 00:25:08.310
- Who was that? - I don't know. They didn't answer.

00:25:08.390 --> 00:25:11.620
But I was told there were only two men there.

00:25:11.680 --> 00:25:13.630
You've got to tell your story to the police.

00:25:13.710 --> 00:25:15.660
Police? Why, police?

00:25:15.740 --> 00:25:20.660
It's nonsense. It is all nonsense. It was an accident.

00:25:20.740 --> 00:25:22.340
You don't know it was an accident.

00:25:22.400 --> 00:25:24.490
You only saw a dead man with three men carrying it.

00:25:24.570 --> 00:25:27.960
The army moved in a ganz step, but I should have listened to my wife.

00:25:28.040 --> 00:25:30.000
She said you were up to no good.

00:25:30.030 --> 00:25:32.370
- Suppose I take your evidence to the police.

00:25:43.370 --> 00:25:44.410
- I have no evidence.

00:25:44.410 --> 00:25:46.330
I saw nothing, I said nothing.

00:25:46.330 --> 00:25:47.330
It's not my business.

00:25:47.330 --> 00:25:49.060
- We'll make it your business.

00:25:59.890 --> 00:26:00.000
it.

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:01.740
You must not bring this gentleman again.

00:26:02.060 --> 00:26:03.400
You must go at once, please.

00:26:03.820 --> 00:26:05.920
Please, sonst vergisse mein wienerischen Charme.

00:26:06.120 --> 00:26:06.420
Please.

00:26:10.990 --> 00:26:16.180
You shouldn't get mixed up in this.

00:26:17.990 --> 00:26:19.470
Well, if I do find out something,

00:26:20.210 --> 00:26:21.240
can I look you up again?

00:26:21.860 --> 00:26:23.160
Why don't you leave this town?

00:26:23.860 --> 00:26:24.200
Go home.

00:26:24.200 --> 00:26:25.000
Unglaublich!

00:26:25.000 --> 00:26:26.780
Ja, wo stecken Sie Sie, Frau Hanschmidt?

00:26:27.680 --> 00:26:29.090
Die Polizei ist oben.

00:26:29.430 --> 00:26:30.000
Sie suchen nach...

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:34.000
and read all of their letters as if you were a prophet.

00:26:34.000 --> 00:26:35.000
What is it?

00:26:35.000 --> 00:26:37.000
What do you want the police?

00:26:37.000 --> 00:26:38.000
I don't know.

00:26:38.000 --> 00:26:39.000
You have to know that.

00:26:39.000 --> 00:26:42.000
I mean, without a reason, the police will not get into the house,

00:26:42.000 --> 00:26:43.820
Mrs. Schmidt.

00:26:43.820 --> 00:26:45.750
What is she talking about?

00:26:45.750 --> 00:26:47.640
The police. They are searching my room.

00:26:47.640 --> 00:26:49.640
You have to know what it is about.

00:26:49.640 --> 00:26:51.640
My God, that is terrible.

00:26:51.640 --> 00:26:54.640
You are not the king in your own house,

00:26:54.640 --> 00:26:57.490
if it would be the Austrian police.

00:26:57.490 --> 00:26:59.910
Amerikaner, Russen.

00:27:00.060 --> 00:27:05.110
and in the night, I mean, if they were to understand myself before.

00:27:06.110 --> 00:27:18.830
What the devil?

00:27:18.830 --> 00:27:20.360
Getting around, Martins?

00:27:20.360 --> 00:27:23.340
Oh, pinning things on girls now.

00:27:23.340 --> 00:27:26.280
Miss Schmidt, I should like to see your papers, please.

00:27:26.280 --> 00:27:27.440
Won't you give him anything?

00:27:30.760 --> 00:27:39.440
- Thank you. You were born in Graz of Austrian parents? - Yes.

00:27:39.940 --> 00:27:58.100
- Very good, sir, isn't he? - Very good, sir.

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:01.440
How much did you pay for this?

00:28:01.440 --> 00:28:06.040
I'm afraid I shall have to keep this for a while, Miss Schmidt.

00:28:06.040 --> 00:28:08.040
How do you expect her to live in this city without her papers?

00:28:08.040 --> 00:28:11.540
Write her out her receipt, Payne, and give her a receipt of those letters, too.

00:28:11.540 --> 00:28:12.450
This way, Miss.

00:28:12.450 --> 00:28:15.560
I suppose it wouldn't interest you to know that Harry Lyme was murdered.

00:28:15.560 --> 00:28:18.420
You're too busy. You haven't even bothered to get the complete evidence.

00:28:18.420 --> 00:28:28.030
Must you take those?

00:28:28.030 --> 00:28:29.320
They'll be returned, Miss.

00:28:30.830 --> 00:28:34.710
- They have private letters. - That's all right, miss. Don't worry.

00:28:34.750 --> 00:28:37.430
We're used to it. Like doctors.

00:28:37.730 --> 00:28:41.700
There was a third man there. I suppose that doesn't sound peculiar to you.

00:28:41.800 --> 00:28:46.710
I'm not interested in whether a racketeer like Lime was killed by his friends or by an accident.

00:28:46.880 --> 00:28:49.010
The only important thing is that he's dead.

00:28:50.440 --> 00:28:53.720
I'm sorry. Tactful too, aren't we, Callaghan?

00:28:53.880 --> 00:28:54.960
Calloway.

00:28:55.060 --> 00:28:57.460
- Must you take those letters? - Yes, I'm afraid so.

00:28:57.760 --> 00:28:59.800
- They're Harry's. - That's the reason.

00:29:00.730 --> 00:29:02.640
You won't learn anything from them.

00:29:02.970 --> 00:29:06.470
They're only love letters. There are not many of them.

00:29:06.650 --> 00:29:09.550
They'll be returned to you, Miss Schmidt, as soon as they've been examined.

00:29:09.930 --> 00:29:13.480
There's nothing in them. Harry never did anything.

00:29:14.790 --> 00:29:16.800
Only a small thing once out of kindness.

00:29:17.210 --> 00:29:21.160
- And what was that? - You've got it in your hand.

00:29:21.970 --> 00:29:24.390
- Major Calloway. - Finished?

00:29:24.470 --> 00:29:25.700
- Yes. - Okay.

00:29:26.770 --> 00:29:29.760
You'll have to come with us, Miss Schmidt.

00:29:30.350 --> 00:29:33.390
- You're not locking her up. - Go home, Martins, like a sensible chap.

00:29:33.470 --> 00:29:35.550
You don't know what you're mixing in. Get the next plane.

00:29:35.630 --> 00:29:37.870
As soon as I get to the bottom of this, I'll get the next plane.

00:29:37.950 --> 00:29:40.670
Death's at the bottom of everything, Martins. Leave death to the professionals.

00:29:40.750 --> 00:29:42.750
Mind if I use that line in my next Western?

00:29:42.830 --> 00:29:45.660
- You can't chuck me out. My papers are in order. - Maybe I'll miss.

00:29:45.800 --> 00:29:48.700
- Your seat for the letters. - I don't want it.

00:29:49.420 --> 00:29:51.670
Well, I've got it when you want it, miss.

00:29:55.400 --> 00:29:57.160
Anything really wrong with your papers?

00:30:01.020 --> 00:30:11.030
"They're forged. The Russians would claim me. I come from Czechoslovakia."

00:30:11.030 --> 00:30:13.170
"Wie, die Vandalen!"

00:30:13.170 --> 00:30:16.290
"Hey, Sie, wo schleppen Sie denn das wieder hin?"

00:30:16.290 --> 00:30:20.870
"Mein Gott, Sie können doch nicht das ganze Haus auf den Kopf schleppen, Monsieur Elisabeth."

00:30:20.870 --> 00:30:22.570
"Versteht er auch nicht."

00:30:22.570 --> 00:30:25.670
"Mensch, hier ist doch kein Wirtshaus."

00:30:25.670 --> 00:30:28.420
"Hier ist das Schlafzimmer einer Dame."

00:30:28.420 --> 00:30:30.000
"Frank Schmidt."

00:30:30.350 --> 00:30:32.430
- Explain it to the people. - Yes, yes.

00:30:32.550 --> 00:30:35.350
Just tell them, so they know it too.

00:30:35.470 --> 00:30:39.110
She's only complaining about the way they behave in her house.

00:30:39.230 --> 00:30:41.730
In the old days, princes used to go out and in.

00:30:41.870 --> 00:30:45.130
- It's not even one meter wrong here. - Give me a cigarette.

00:30:45.250 --> 00:30:48.530
That was in the old days. They used to behave decently.

00:30:48.670 --> 00:30:50.520
- Cigarette. - Thank you.

00:30:50.810 --> 00:30:52.010
Thank you, Mr. Liebenshütten.

00:30:52.100 --> 00:30:55.940
Oh, no, thank you very much. You're the only decent person here.

00:30:56.060 --> 00:30:57.520
Miss Schmidt, ready?

00:31:01.180 --> 00:31:07.910
I'll, uh, straighten out all this nonsense about Harry, UPI.

00:31:07.970 --> 00:31:13.350
Sometimes he said I laughed too much.

00:31:13.350 --> 00:31:19.740
Oh, uh, what's the name of that doctor? Harry's doctor?

00:31:19.750 --> 00:31:23.080
- Dr. Winkle. - What do you want to see a doctor for?

00:31:23.200 --> 00:31:25.350
- Hmm? - A bruised lip.

00:31:25.350 --> 00:31:30.000
- Good. - She's not American.

00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:37.970
in every country. The people are sure to take the coups. One thing is to say: the freedom I have made myself very differently.

00:31:37.970 --> 00:31:52.050
Laboratory, we're coming right down. Here, Mr. Schmidt.

00:32:02.490 --> 00:32:27.330
Is Dr. Winkle in?

00:32:27.740 --> 00:32:29.410
The speech time is between 3 and 5.

00:32:30.380 --> 00:32:32.380
- I'm sorry, I don't speak German. - No.

00:32:32.380 --> 00:32:36.580
Please, won't you say that I'm a friend of Harry Lyme?

00:32:36.580 --> 00:32:37.580
Hilde?

00:32:37.580 --> 00:32:39.580
- Yes? - Führen Sie den Herrn herein.

00:32:39.580 --> 00:32:44.560
- Bitte. - Bitte sehr.

00:32:44.560 --> 00:32:59.010
Guten Abend.

00:32:59.010 --> 00:33:00.000
Dr. Winkle?

00:33:00.670 --> 00:33:07.550
Winkel, Dr. Winkel, quite a collection of, uh, collection.

00:33:07.550 --> 00:33:08.840
Yes.

00:33:21.070 --> 00:33:27.250
- Is that your dog? - Yes. Would you mind, Mr. Martins, coming to the point?

00:33:27.250 --> 00:33:30.000
- Please. - Thank you. I have guests waiting.

00:33:30.030 --> 00:33:33.900
- Both friends of Harry Lyne. - I was his medical advisor.

00:33:33.980 --> 00:33:36.570
- I want to find out all I can. - Find out?

00:33:36.650 --> 00:33:40.070
- Hear the details. - I can tell you very little.

00:33:40.150 --> 00:33:44.080
He was run over by a car. He was dead when I arrived.

00:33:44.300 --> 00:33:48.100
- Who was with him? - Two friends of his.

00:33:48.350 --> 00:33:50.780
- Sure? - Two?

00:33:51.010 --> 00:33:53.050
Quite sure.

00:33:53.810 --> 00:33:59.510
- Could he have been at all conscious? - I understand he was, yes, for a short time.

00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:08.040
they carried him across the road. In great pain? Not necessarily. Could he have been capable of

00:34:08.040 --> 00:34:14.270
making plans for me and others just, just during those few moments? I understand he left some

00:34:14.270 --> 00:34:20.510
instructions before he died. I cannot give an opinion. I was not there. My opinion is

00:34:21.230 --> 00:34:30.000
limited to the causes of death. Have you any reason to be dissatisfied? Was it possible

00:34:31.210 --> 00:34:34.840
...that his death might have been not accidental.

00:34:36.360 --> 00:34:42.820
Could he have been pushed, Dr. Winkle?

00:34:44.770 --> 00:34:49.660
Winkle, I cannot give an opinion.

00:34:50.360 --> 00:34:54.160
The injuries to the head and skull would have been the same.

00:35:01.820 --> 00:35:05.540
- Major, may I see you for a moment, please? - Certainly, Brodsky.

00:35:05.560 --> 00:35:06.400
What is it?

00:35:06.570 --> 00:35:11.370
This forgery is very clever, and we are interested in this case.

00:35:11.540 --> 00:35:13.500
- Have you arrested the girl? - No, not yet.

00:35:13.580 --> 00:35:17.520
Please, keep this passport to yourself until I will make some inquiries.

00:35:17.600 --> 00:35:19.520
- Will you, Major? - Yes, of course.

00:35:19.520 --> 00:35:24.520
Sit down, Miss Schmidt. We'll send your letters and things back to you.

00:35:24.900 --> 00:35:28.260
- And my passport? - We'll need that for a while longer.

00:35:33.420 --> 00:35:37.330
- What did he mean? - You know as much as I do.

00:35:38.940 --> 00:35:42.820
Miss Schmidt, you were intimate with Lyme, weren't you?

00:35:43.450 --> 00:35:45.790
We loved each other. You mean that?

00:35:45.930 --> 00:35:49.050
- Do you know this man? - I've never seen him.

00:35:49.140 --> 00:35:51.130
- Joseph Harbin? - No.

00:35:51.240 --> 00:35:53.950
- He works in a military hospital. - No.

00:35:54.220 --> 00:35:59.640
It's stupid to lie to me, Miss Schmidt. I'm in a position to help you.

00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:04.660
I'm not lying. You're wrong about Harry. You're wrong about everything.

00:36:04.740 --> 00:36:09.040
In one of his letters, he asked you to telephone a good friend of his called Joseph.

00:36:09.060 --> 00:36:11.880
He gave you the number of the Casanova Club.

00:36:11.880 --> 00:36:14.970
That's where a lot of friends of Lyme used to go.

00:36:15.050 --> 00:36:19.520
- It wasn't important. - What was the message?

00:36:19.590 --> 00:36:23.270
Something about meeting Harry at his home.

00:36:23.330 --> 00:36:25.720
Harbin disappeared the day you telephoned.

00:36:25.760 --> 00:36:29.340
We've got to find him. You can help us.

00:36:31.720 --> 00:36:35.990
- What can I tell you, but you've got everything upside down.

00:36:35.990 --> 00:36:37.490
- Okay.

00:36:37.490 --> 00:36:41.570
That American friend of yours is still waiting for you.

00:36:41.570 --> 00:36:46.780
He won't do you much good.

00:36:46.780 --> 00:36:47.700
- I thank you, Miss Smith.

00:36:47.700 --> 00:36:49.240
We'll send for you when we want you.

00:36:57.860 --> 00:37:00.000
- Mr. Martin's, I've been trying to get you with your

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:02.000
I've arranged that lecture for tomorrow.

00:37:02.000 --> 00:37:03.000
Oh, what about?

00:37:03.000 --> 00:37:06.000
On the modern novel, you remember what we arranged?

00:37:06.000 --> 00:37:08.000
Now, they want you to talk about the crisis of fate.

00:37:08.000 --> 00:37:09.000
What's that?

00:37:09.000 --> 00:37:10.920
Oh, I thought you'd know you're a writer.

00:37:10.920 --> 00:37:12.920
Oh, but of course you do. Good night, old man.

00:37:12.920 --> 00:37:15.920
Oh, I've forgotten my hat. I'll let you know the time later.

00:37:15.920 --> 00:37:16.840
Train?

00:37:16.840 --> 00:37:27.160
Whiskey.

00:37:27.160 --> 00:37:28.720
Two whiskies.

00:37:35.530 --> 00:37:37.110
It's my biscuit. How much?

00:37:37.110 --> 00:37:38.110
It's months a shilling bidder.

00:37:38.110 --> 00:37:39.540
Well, they don't take army money here.

00:37:39.540 --> 00:37:44.270
- How much did he say? - Harry?

00:37:44.270 --> 00:37:49.400
Yes. He moved his head, but the rest is good, isn't it?

00:38:06.010 --> 00:38:08.160
- Good evening, Miss Schmidt. - Good evening.

00:38:08.160 --> 00:38:12.160
You have found out my little secret. The man must leave.

00:38:12.160 --> 00:38:16.610
How goes the investigation? Have you proved the policeman wrong?

00:38:16.640 --> 00:38:18.370
- Not yet. - But you will.

00:38:18.630 --> 00:38:23.200
Our friend, Dr. Winkel, said you had called. Wasn't he helpful?

00:38:23.200 --> 00:38:27.040
- Well, he was limited. - But Mr. Popescu is here tonight.

00:38:27.120 --> 00:38:30.000
- The Romanian? - Yes, the man who helped carry him.

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:35.360
- He's back now. - Well, I'd like to meet all of Harry's friends.

00:38:35.480 --> 00:38:39.470
- I'll bring him to you. - Haven't you done enough for tonight?

00:38:39.630 --> 00:38:43.700
- Porter said three men carried the body and two of them a year.

00:38:43.860 --> 00:38:53.560
- Who are you looking for now? - Stop.

00:39:00.640 --> 00:39:03.020
- Mr. Popesco, Mr. Martins. - How do you do?

00:39:03.040 --> 00:39:05.180
- Good. - And a friend of Harry's, a friend of mine.

00:39:05.260 --> 00:39:07.400
- I'll leave you together. - Good evening, Schmidt.

00:39:07.440 --> 00:39:09.280
- You remember me? - Of course.

00:39:09.850 --> 00:39:13.200
- I helped Harry fix her papers, Mr. Martins. - Oh, you did.

00:39:13.280 --> 00:39:15.280
Not a sort of thing I should confess to a stranger,

00:39:15.360 --> 00:39:18.210
but you have to break the rules sometimes.

00:39:18.270 --> 00:39:20.100
Humanity is a duty.

00:39:20.260 --> 00:39:22.040
- Cigarette, Schmidt? - Yes, danke.

00:39:22.120 --> 00:39:23.070
Keep the pack.

00:39:23.370 --> 00:39:26.500
I understand you were with Harry. Two double biscuits.

00:39:26.530 --> 00:39:29.730
- And what do you think? - Thank you.

00:39:31.500 --> 00:39:35.470
It was a terrible thing. I was just crossing the road to go to Harry.

00:39:36.070 --> 00:39:38.330
He and the Baron were on the sidewalk.

00:39:39.160 --> 00:39:41.920
Maybe if I hadn't started to cross the road, it wouldn't have happened.

00:39:42.530 --> 00:39:46.120
I can't help blaming myself and wishing things had been different.

00:39:47.190 --> 00:39:51.540
Anyway, he saw me and stepped off the sidewalk to meet me.

00:39:52.660 --> 00:39:56.400
And the truck, it was terrible, Mr. Martins, terrible.

00:39:56.840 --> 00:39:58.480
I've never seen a man killed before.

00:39:59.100 --> 00:40:00.000
I think there was something...

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:03.020
- What's funny about the whole thing? - Funny?

00:40:03.120 --> 00:40:07.400
- Something wrong. - Of course I was. Some eyes for Mr. Martins.

00:40:07.500 --> 00:40:11.580
- You think so, too? - It was so terribly stupid for a man like Harry...

00:40:11.680 --> 00:40:15.660
- ...to be killed in an ordinary street accident. - That's all you meant?

00:40:15.760 --> 00:40:20.250
- What else? - Who was the third man?

00:40:20.350 --> 00:40:24.460
I oughtn't to drink it. It makes me acid.

00:40:24.560 --> 00:40:30.000
- What man would you be referring to, Mr. Martins? - I was told that the third man helped you and Kurtz carry the body.

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:33.250
I don't know how you got that idea. You'll find all about it in the police report.

00:40:33.300 --> 00:40:36.150
There were just the two of us, me and the Baron.

00:40:36.150 --> 00:40:38.770
Who could have told you a story like that?

00:40:38.800 --> 00:40:41.780
It was at Harry's place. He was cleaning the window at the time.

00:40:41.780 --> 00:40:45.560
- And so the accident? - No, no, he didn't see the accident.

00:40:45.590 --> 00:40:48.050
But he saw three men carrying the body.

00:40:48.170 --> 00:40:52.400
- Why wasn't he at the police inquiry? - He didn't want to get involved.

00:40:52.420 --> 00:40:55.220
You'll never teach these Austrians to be good citizens.

00:40:55.220 --> 00:41:00.000
It was his duty to give the evidence. Even so, he remembers wrong.

00:41:01.120 --> 00:41:06.650
- What else did he tell you? - Harry was dead before you got him to that statue.

00:41:06.650 --> 00:41:10.170
He probably knows a lot more than that. Somebody's lying.

00:41:10.170 --> 00:41:15.040
- Not necessarily. - Police say he was mixed up in some racket?

00:41:15.040 --> 00:41:19.510
Oh, that's quite impossible. He had a great sense of duty.

00:41:19.510 --> 00:41:21.810
Friend Kurt seems to think it was possible.

00:41:21.810 --> 00:41:26.130
I understand how an Anglo-Saxon feels. The Baron hasn't heard it, you know.

00:41:26.130 --> 00:41:30.000
He seems to have been around a bit. Do you know a man...

00:41:30.000 --> 00:41:32.020
- A man called... - Haben.

00:41:32.020 --> 00:41:35.300
- Joseph Haben. - Joseph, huh?

00:41:35.300 --> 00:41:41.880
That's a nice girl, that.

00:41:41.880 --> 00:41:43.880
But she ought to go careful in Vienna.

00:41:43.880 --> 00:41:48.460
Everybody ought to go careful in a city like this.

00:41:48.460 --> 00:41:53.320
Kilometers at the bridge.

00:41:53.320 --> 00:41:54.320
Good.

00:42:31.760 --> 00:42:35.590
Hello. Is it so very important for you?

00:42:35.620 --> 00:42:36.640
Yes, it is.

00:42:36.720 --> 00:42:40.720
I am not a bad man. I'd like to tell you something.

00:42:40.840 --> 00:42:42.640
Tell me, how did the car...

00:42:42.890 --> 00:42:46.700
Come tonight. My wife tours out.

00:42:46.780 --> 00:42:48.690
All right, I'll come back, but...

00:42:49.000 --> 00:42:49.940
Tonight.

00:43:05.370 --> 00:43:08.210
- Barista? - Does that mean come in?

00:43:09.080 --> 00:43:11.890
- Oh, yes, yes, come in. - The reporter's going to talk to us tonight.

00:43:12.070 --> 00:43:13.830
Need we go through it all again.

00:43:13.920 --> 00:43:16.550
I didn't manage by myself. Are you busy?

00:43:17.190 --> 00:43:19.170
Just another part I've got to learn.

00:43:19.350 --> 00:43:22.370
- Can I hear you? - In German?

00:43:22.440 --> 00:43:25.590
I try. Is it comedy or tragedy?

00:43:25.650 --> 00:43:27.420
Comedy. I don't play tragedy.

00:43:27.620 --> 00:43:29.540
- Do I read... - Well, you read these.

00:43:33.960 --> 00:43:36.260
I guess didn't find Harrigan.

00:43:36.260 --> 00:43:37.630
What's that?

00:43:37.630 --> 00:43:39.850
Uh, Harrigan, I guess.

00:43:39.850 --> 00:43:41.850
H-E-Z-T...

00:43:41.850 --> 00:43:42.850
Oh, let me see.

00:43:42.850 --> 00:43:43.850
No, no, no, that's not the cue.

00:43:43.850 --> 00:43:47.430
It means she has to sit down.

00:43:47.430 --> 00:43:54.170
Hausmann.

00:43:54.170 --> 00:43:59.730
No, no, it's no good.

00:44:02.750 --> 00:44:04.830
It's always bad about this time.

00:44:04.830 --> 00:44:08.240
He was still waking around six.

00:44:08.240 --> 00:44:09.240
I've been frightened.

00:44:09.240 --> 00:44:14.690
I've been alone, without friends and money.

00:44:14.690 --> 00:44:17.890
I've never known anything like this.

00:44:17.890 --> 00:44:21.690
Please talk.

00:44:21.690 --> 00:44:23.090
Tell me about him.

00:44:23.090 --> 00:44:24.090
Tell me what?

00:44:24.090 --> 00:44:25.620
Oh, anything.

00:44:25.620 --> 00:44:26.620
Just talk.

00:44:26.620 --> 00:44:28.460
Where did you see him last?

00:44:28.460 --> 00:44:28.970
When?

00:44:28.970 --> 00:44:30.000
What did you do?

00:44:31.370 --> 00:44:38.160
- Oh, we didn't make much sense, we drank too much once they tried to steal my girl. - There she is.

00:44:38.160 --> 00:44:42.910
- Oh, that was nine years ago. - Tell me more.

00:44:42.910 --> 00:44:49.600
It's very difficult. You knew Harry. We didn't do anything very amusing.

00:44:49.600 --> 00:44:52.520
Just made everything seem like such, uh, fun.

00:44:52.520 --> 00:44:54.450
Was he clever when he was a boy?

00:44:54.450 --> 00:44:57.150
I suppose so. He could fix anything.

00:44:57.150 --> 00:45:00.000
- What sort of things? - Old little things.

00:45:00.160 --> 00:45:06.240
Which temperature are up before exam, the best crib, how to avoid this and that.

00:45:06.320 --> 00:45:07.740
He fixed my papers for me.

00:45:09.260 --> 00:45:13.060
He heard the Russians were repatriating people like me who came from Czechoslovakia.

00:45:14.490 --> 00:45:16.950
He knew the right person straight away for forging stamps.

00:45:18.310 --> 00:45:21.020
When he was 14, he taught me the three-card trick.

00:45:21.930 --> 00:45:23.010
That's growing up fast.

00:45:23.730 --> 00:45:24.770
He never grew up.

00:45:26.370 --> 00:45:28.310
The world grew up around him, that's all.

00:45:30.960 --> 00:45:36.760
- And buried him. - Not again.

00:45:37.580 --> 00:45:41.810
You see, I don't want to. I don't ever want to.

00:45:42.510 --> 00:45:46.520
- Come on out and have a drink. - Why did you say that?

00:45:46.690 --> 00:45:51.280
- Seemed like a good idea. - It's just what he has to say.

00:45:52.030 --> 00:45:55.120
Well, I did learn that from him.

00:45:56.090 --> 00:45:59.020
If we have to see the porter, we'd better go.

00:45:59.120 --> 00:46:00.000
Sorry, Cartwright.

00:46:00.000 --> 00:46:02.800
- Can we talk quietly for a couple of minutes? - I thought you wanted to...

00:46:02.800 --> 00:46:04.710
A moment ago you said you didn't want to see the poor.

00:46:04.710 --> 00:46:08.520
- We're both in it, Harry. - Holly.

00:46:08.520 --> 00:46:14.010
- I'm so sorry. - It's all right.

00:46:14.010 --> 00:46:17.470
Might get my name right.

00:46:17.470 --> 00:46:22.390
You know, you ought to find yourself a girl.

00:46:22.390 --> 00:46:24.910
His English is so very bad we'll let him talk German.

00:46:24.910 --> 00:46:27.030
- You'll just be good enough to translate. - Look.

00:46:30.120 --> 00:46:33.780
- That's Harry's place, isn't it? - Yes. Let's go away.

00:46:34.700 --> 00:46:37.360
- What's the matter? - Let's not get into any more trouble.

00:46:38.410 --> 00:46:44.970
Wait here. What's the matter?

00:46:44.980 --> 00:46:49.270
- It's close. - The portier is over.

00:46:49.370 --> 00:46:53.770
- I don't understand. - The porter is dead.

00:46:53.870 --> 00:46:59.320
The porter is dead.

00:47:00.220 --> 00:47:28.070
I don't understand.

00:47:39.400 --> 00:47:42.310
- What is it? - It would have been murdered.

00:47:42.310 --> 00:47:45.550
When they assist him in what Yiddish try to write.

00:47:45.550 --> 00:47:48.870
- Wahrscheinlich he's dead or murdered. - I think you did it.

00:47:48.870 --> 00:47:58.760
Murder! Murder! Murder! Murder!

00:47:58.760 --> 00:48:00.000
- Mama! - Yes, Mama!

00:48:06.140 --> 00:48:08.640
Hello? What are you doing?

00:48:08.680 --> 00:48:10.690
Hello? Wait a minute!

00:48:18.080 --> 00:48:19.620
Stop right there!

00:48:53.260 --> 00:48:55.460
I don't want to talk about that.

00:48:55.460 --> 00:48:56.460
But yesterday was...

00:48:56.460 --> 00:48:58.380
Yes, but you know what happened.

00:48:58.380 --> 00:49:00.000
It's not going to go back.

00:49:00.030 --> 00:49:03.150
- Do you think I will not see you again? - What are you going to do?

00:49:03.420 --> 00:49:06.180
- I wish I knew. - Be sensible. Tell Major Calloway.

00:49:27.220 --> 00:49:30.000
Get me Major Callahan on the phone.

00:49:30.000 --> 00:49:33.200
- No, Mr. Martins, excuse me. - It's very urgent. Just get him on the telephone.

00:49:33.300 --> 00:49:35.500
- Do you know his number? - No, I don't know his number.

00:49:35.600 --> 00:49:38.500
- I look it up. - Is there a car here I can use?

00:49:38.600 --> 00:49:41.680
Of course. They are not waiting for you.

00:49:41.740 --> 00:49:44.880
- Be tuned, Herr. Be tuned. - Never mind about the number.

00:49:47.870 --> 00:49:54.160
Take me to the headquarters.

00:50:01.080 --> 00:50:03.080
- Let's go.

00:50:03.080 --> 00:50:06.300
Driver! Driver!

00:50:06.300 --> 00:50:15.450
You want us to kill me?

00:50:42.920 --> 00:50:44.180
Pitching gets rein here.

00:50:49.480 --> 00:50:52.320
Ah, Mr. Martins, what a relief to see you.

00:50:52.520 --> 00:50:54.360
I was beginning to think something had happened to you.

00:50:54.460 --> 00:50:56.760
Come along, Mr. Martins, everything's ready for you.

00:50:57.120 --> 00:51:00.000
I was frantic in case you hadn't got my message of the hope.

00:51:00.000 --> 00:51:03.180
A quarter's out here is so unreliable, if you know what I mean.

00:51:03.260 --> 00:51:05.610
We're all set for a wonderful meeting.

00:51:05.740 --> 00:51:08.000
You'll find the audience most appreciative.

00:51:08.080 --> 00:51:10.200
- Oh, let me take your coat. - I've got it.

00:51:10.280 --> 00:51:13.920
There'll be the freshmen's afterwards. Come along, Mr. Hamm. Follow me.

00:51:14.000 --> 00:51:16.580
Well, here we are, ladies and gentlemen. All's well that ends well.

00:51:16.660 --> 00:51:18.380
- Would you look up for those for me? - Thank you.

00:51:18.460 --> 00:51:20.260
Would you like to sit there, Mr. Martins?

00:51:20.270 --> 00:51:22.400
That's right. Ladies and gentlemen, I have much pleasure...

00:51:22.480 --> 00:51:26.280
in introducing Mr. Holly Martins from the other side.

00:51:30.830 --> 00:51:47.850
- Well, bring the car and anyone else would like to come. Don't be long. - Suppose that is what I meant to say.

00:51:47.850 --> 00:51:52.850
- Of course, of course, of course. - Do you believe Mr. Martins in the stream of consciousness?

00:51:52.850 --> 00:52:00.000
- The stream of consciousness? - Well, uh, well, uh...

00:52:00.000 --> 00:52:01.960
has chiefly influenced you.

00:52:02.040 --> 00:52:04.970
- Gray. - Gray? What gray?

00:52:05.050 --> 00:52:08.150
- Zane Gray. - Oh, that's Mr. Martin's little joke, of course.

00:52:08.230 --> 00:52:12.150
We all know perfectly well Zane Gray wrote what we call westerns.

00:52:12.230 --> 00:52:13.740
Cowboys and bandits.

00:52:13.820 --> 00:52:17.740
Mr. James Joyce, now, where would you put him?

00:52:17.820 --> 00:52:22.580
Would you mind repeating that question?

00:52:22.660 --> 00:52:27.220
I said, where would you put Mr. James Joyce? In what category?

00:52:27.300 --> 00:52:30.000
Can I ask, is Mr. Martin's engaged?

00:52:30.000 --> 00:52:36.800
- Yes, it's called "The Third Man." - A novel, Mr. Martins?

00:52:36.800 --> 00:52:41.610
- It's a murder story. I've just started it. It's based on fact.

00:52:41.610 --> 00:52:46.630
- Why, it's Mr. Popesco. Oh, very great pleasure to see you here, Mr. Popesco.

00:52:46.630 --> 00:52:51.630
As you know, ladies and gentlemen, Mr. Popesco is a very great supporter of one of our medical charities.

00:52:51.630 --> 00:52:56.240
- Are you a slow writer, Mr. Martins? - Not when I get interested.

00:52:56.240 --> 00:53:00.000
I see you were doing something pretty dangerous this night.

00:53:00.000 --> 00:53:03.580
- Yes? - Mixing fact and fiction.

00:53:03.580 --> 00:53:06.660
- Should I make it all back? - Why no, Mr. Martins.

00:53:06.660 --> 00:53:09.660
I'd say stick to fiction, straight fiction.

00:53:09.660 --> 00:53:12.520
I'm too far along with the book, Mr. Popesco.

00:53:12.520 --> 00:53:15.520
Haven't you ever scrapped a book, Mr. Martins?

00:53:15.520 --> 00:53:18.930
- Never. - Pity.

00:53:18.930 --> 00:53:22.090
Ladies and gentlemen, if there are no more questions for Mr. Martins,

00:53:22.090 --> 00:53:25.640
I think I can call the meeting officially closed.

00:55:17.820 --> 00:55:23.340
to go away martin this isn't santa fe i'm not a sheriff and you aren't a cowboy you've been

00:55:23.340 --> 00:55:26.870
blundering around with the worst bunch of racketeers in vienna you're precious harry's

00:55:26.870 --> 00:55:30.000
friends and now you're wanted for murder put down drunken disorderly too

00:55:30.000 --> 00:55:33.760
- I have. What's the matter with your hand? - Parrot bit me.

00:55:33.840 --> 00:55:36.310
Stop behaving like a fool, Martins.

00:55:36.480 --> 00:55:39.830
I'm only a little fool. I'm an amateur at it. You're a professional.

00:55:39.960 --> 00:55:42.270
You've been shaking your cap and bells all over town.

00:55:42.350 --> 00:55:46.330
Payne, get me the Harry Lime file and get Mr. Martins a large whiskey.

00:55:46.490 --> 00:55:49.910
I don't need your drinks, Callaway. You will.

00:55:49.950 --> 00:55:52.910
I don't want another murder in this case, and you were born to be murdered.

00:55:52.990 --> 00:55:56.500
- So you're going to hear the facts. - You haven't told me a single one yet.

00:55:56.500 --> 00:55:59.890
Have you ever heard of penicillin?

00:56:00.760 --> 00:56:03.660
Well, in Vienna, there hasn't been enough penicillin to go around.

00:56:04.010 --> 00:56:05.670
So a nice trade started here.

00:56:07.080 --> 00:56:09.080
Stealing penicillin from the military hospitals,

00:56:10.220 --> 00:56:13.650
diluting it to make it go further, and selling it to patients.

00:56:13.850 --> 00:56:14.770
Do you see what that means?

00:56:15.110 --> 00:56:19.030
Are you too busy chasing a few tubes of penicillin to investigate a murder?

00:56:19.210 --> 00:56:20.150
These were murders.

00:56:21.510 --> 00:56:26.300
Men with gangrened legs, women in childbirth, and there were children, too.

00:56:27.100 --> 00:56:30.000
They used some of this diluted penicillin against meningitis.

00:56:30.440 --> 00:56:33.820
The lucky children died. The unlucky ones went off their heads.

00:56:33.920 --> 00:56:35.790
You can see them now in the mental ward.

00:56:36.190 --> 00:56:38.390
That was the racket Harry Lyme organized.

00:56:38.670 --> 00:56:41.810
Calloway, you haven't shown me one shred of evidence.

00:56:41.910 --> 00:56:43.240
We're just coming to that.

00:56:43.870 --> 00:56:46.670
Payne, Magic Lantern Show.

00:56:47.830 --> 00:56:50.720
- Be good, sir. - You know, Payne's one of your devoted readers.

00:56:50.910 --> 00:56:52.860
He's promised to lend me one of your books.

00:56:53.120 --> 00:56:55.700
- Which one is it, Payne? - The Lone Rider of Santa Fe, sir.

00:56:55.800 --> 00:57:00.000
- That's right. The Lone Rider of Santa Fe. - I'd like to visit Texas one day, sir.

00:57:00.250 --> 00:57:01.800
Come on, show me what you've got to show.

00:57:02.110 --> 00:57:06.900
- All right, then? - Yes, sir.

00:57:07.330 --> 00:57:10.680
- Pain, pain, pain. - I got them muddled.

00:57:10.690 --> 00:57:13.580
It's the new lot that's just coming for Mr. Crabbin.

00:57:13.600 --> 00:57:16.870
See this man here?

00:57:17.550 --> 00:57:21.120
A fellow called Harbin, a medical orderly at the General Hospital.

00:57:21.560 --> 00:57:24.770
He worked for Lyme and helped to steal the stuff from the laboratories.

00:57:24.980 --> 00:57:29.760
We forced him to give information to us, which led us as far as Kurtz and Lyme.

00:57:30.320 --> 00:57:32.520
But we didn't arrest them, as our evidence wasn't complete...

00:57:32.620 --> 00:57:34.820
and it might have spoiled our chances of getting the others.

00:57:34.920 --> 00:57:38.220
- Next pane. - I'd like a word with this orderly hobbin.

00:57:38.320 --> 00:57:40.740
- So would I. - Well, bring him in.

00:57:40.900 --> 00:57:43.000
I can't. He disappeared a week ago.

00:57:43.100 --> 00:57:45.300
It's more like a mortuary than police headquarters.

00:57:45.400 --> 00:57:49.520
We have better witnesses. Look here.

00:58:02.080 --> 00:58:22.660
Go back to the hotel and duke you out of trouble.

00:58:22.660 --> 00:58:25.220
I'll try and fix things with the Austrian police.

00:58:25.220 --> 00:58:29.220
You'll be all right in the hotel, but I can't be responsible for you on the streets.

00:58:30.090 --> 00:58:41.180
asking you to. I'm sorry, Martins. I'm sorry, too. Still got an airplane ticket on you. We'll send one

00:58:41.180 --> 00:58:49.150
across to your hotel in the morning. Thank you. Get me Austrian police headquarters. Can I have that

00:58:49.150 --> 00:58:56.740
woman's passport? You know, the Anna Schmidt one. Oh, we're not going to pick her after that, are we?

00:58:56.900 --> 00:59:00.000
What can we do? We have our instructions.

00:59:00.960 --> 00:59:03.040
¶¶

00:59:44.780 --> 00:59:45.460
But this...

01:00:02.300 --> 01:00:05.510
- What is it? What's happened to you? - I just came to see you.

01:00:06.910 --> 01:00:09.640
- Come in. - I thought you were going to keep away.

01:00:09.720 --> 01:00:12.980
- Are the police after you? - I don't know.

01:00:14.590 --> 01:00:18.210
- Your turn, character. - A bit. I'm sorry.

01:00:20.540 --> 01:00:25.600
But I did want to say goodbye before I pushed off and went back home.

01:00:25.810 --> 01:00:30.000
- Why? - It's what you've always wanted.

01:00:31.020 --> 01:00:37.660
Kitty. Here, Kitty. Kitty. Here, Kitty. Kitty, Kitty, Kitty.

01:00:37.660 --> 01:00:42.260
Do you want to play, Kitty? Sleepy? Sleepy, Kitty?

01:00:42.260 --> 01:00:44.730
Not very sociable, is he?

01:00:44.730 --> 01:00:46.750
No, he only liked Harry.

01:00:46.750 --> 01:00:50.430
What made you decide so suddenly?

01:00:50.430 --> 01:00:53.870
I brought you these. They got a little wet.

01:00:53.870 --> 01:00:54.870
What happened to your hand?

01:00:54.870 --> 01:00:58.700
Harry, let it go.

01:00:58.700 --> 01:01:00.000
Have you seen Cat?

01:01:02.360 --> 01:01:05.570
- Am I an apparent nipping a man? - Have you?

01:01:05.570 --> 01:01:07.550
It's...

01:01:07.550 --> 01:01:13.890
Oh, I've been saying goodbye all over.

01:01:13.890 --> 01:01:16.460
He told you, didn't he?

01:01:16.460 --> 01:01:20.280
- Told me? - About Harry.

01:01:20.280 --> 01:01:25.380
- Do you know? - I've seen Major Callaway today.

01:02:08.090 --> 01:02:13.390
I knew he was mixed up but not like that.

01:02:13.390 --> 01:02:16.460
I knew him for 20 years. At least I thought I knew him.

01:02:16.460 --> 01:02:20.470
I suppose he was laughing at fools like us all the time.

01:02:20.470 --> 01:02:23.970
- He liked to laugh. - 70 pounds or two.

01:02:23.970 --> 01:02:29.440
He wanted me to write for his great medical charity.

01:02:30.000 --> 01:02:31.300
I'll put this flowers in the water.

01:02:32.060 --> 01:02:35.660
Perhaps I could have raised the price to 80 pounds for him.

01:02:35.760 --> 01:02:39.160
Oh, please, for heaven's sake, stop making him in your image.

01:02:39.970 --> 01:02:40.770
Harry was real.

01:02:41.070 --> 01:02:43.050
He wasn't just your friend and my lover.

01:02:43.270 --> 01:02:44.010
He was Harry.

01:02:44.170 --> 01:02:46.740
Well, don't preach wisdom to me.

01:02:47.710 --> 01:02:52.210
You talk about him as if he had occasional bad manners.

01:02:52.940 --> 01:02:54.220
Oh, I don't know.

01:02:54.220 --> 01:03:00.000
I'm just a hack writer who drinks too much and falls in love with girls.

01:03:03.040 --> 01:03:05.960
- You? - Me?

01:03:05.960 --> 01:03:08.450
Don't be such a fool, of course.

01:03:08.450 --> 01:03:13.060
If you'd rang me up and asked me whether you're fair or dark,

01:03:13.060 --> 01:03:16.110
if I had a moustache, I wouldn't have known.

01:03:16.110 --> 01:03:18.130
I am leaving the other.

01:03:18.130 --> 01:03:24.570
I don't care whether Harry was murdered by Kurtz or Popesco or the third man.

01:03:24.570 --> 01:03:29.280
Whoever killed him, there was some sort of justice.

01:03:30.320 --> 01:03:32.340
Maybe I would have killed him myself.

01:03:32.600 --> 01:03:34.840
A person doesn't change because you find out more.

01:03:35.370 --> 01:03:40.070
Look, I've got a splitting headache and you stand there and just talk and talk and talk.

01:03:40.170 --> 01:03:41.070
I hate it.

01:03:42.180 --> 01:03:45.730
First time I ever saw you laugh.

01:03:46.500 --> 01:03:47.710
Do it again.

01:03:53.710 --> 01:03:55.230
It isn't enough for two loves.

01:04:02.240 --> 01:04:12.380
I'd make comic faces and stand on my head and grin at you between my legs and

01:04:12.380 --> 01:04:22.020
lots of jokes. I wouldn't stand a chance, would I?

01:04:37.870 --> 01:04:42.360
You did tell me I find myself a girl.

01:05:11.840 --> 01:05:17.670
What kind of a spy do you think you are, Satchelfutt?

01:05:17.670 --> 01:05:22.690
What are you tailing me for?

01:05:22.690 --> 01:05:26.230
That's your tongue.

01:05:26.230 --> 01:05:28.560
Come on out.

01:05:28.560 --> 01:05:30.000
Come out, come out.

01:05:30.000 --> 01:05:36.950
Step out in the light and let's have a look at it.

01:05:36.950 --> 01:05:38.130
Who's your boss?

01:05:52.860 --> 01:05:54.770
Mary!

01:06:00.000 --> 01:06:00.380
Thank you.

01:07:09.470 --> 01:07:17.810
Followed his shadow, and suddenly, this is where he vanished.

01:07:17.890 --> 01:07:21.740
- I suppose you don't believe me? - No.

01:07:21.820 --> 01:07:24.710
I tell you, you don't think I'm blind, do you?

01:07:24.790 --> 01:07:27.130
Yes. Where were you when you saw him first?

01:07:27.210 --> 01:07:30.000
- Fifty yards right down there. - Which side of the road?

01:07:30.280 --> 01:07:33.760
I was on that side, his shadow was on that side, and no turnings on either side.

01:07:33.820 --> 01:07:34.740
What about the doorways?

01:07:35.060 --> 01:07:36.560
I tell you, I heard him running ahead of me.

01:07:36.640 --> 01:07:43.240
Yes, yes, yes, and then he vanished out there, I suppose, with a puff of smoke and like a clap of...

01:08:00.030 --> 01:08:01.000
German gin.

01:08:30.000 --> 01:08:34.100
- What's this? Where are we? - It's the main sewer.

01:08:34.130 --> 01:08:39.600
Runs right into the Blue Danube. Smells sweet, doesn't it?

01:08:39.600 --> 01:08:45.300
We should have dug deeper than engraved.

01:09:06.400 --> 01:09:11.140
-

01:09:33.450 --> 01:09:38.260
- Do you know him, Major? - Yes.

01:09:39.480 --> 01:09:43.890
Yes, Joseph Harbin, medical orderly at the General Hospital.

01:09:44.880 --> 01:09:49.090
- He used to work for Harry Lyme. - Joseph Harbin?

01:09:49.560 --> 01:09:53.460
Yes, he's the man I told you was missing.

01:09:55.010 --> 01:09:57.620
Next time we'll have a foolproof coffin.

01:10:09.760 --> 01:10:12.680
- Passport and arrest warrant. - Yes, sir.

01:10:13.800 --> 01:10:24.250
I can't run like this.

01:10:24.370 --> 01:10:28.130
It's an awful house, not a barrack.

01:10:37.680 --> 01:10:38.860
- Where's the mafia?

01:10:38.860 --> 01:10:39.880
- Teresa.

01:10:39.880 --> 01:10:41.520
- Internationale Policai.

01:11:00.510 --> 01:11:04.430
- Mrs. Schmidt? - Yes, what do you want?

01:11:04.550 --> 01:11:07.580
- You have to come with us. - Why?

01:11:07.720 --> 01:11:12.660
- Is this your passport? - Yes.

01:11:12.780 --> 01:11:14.980
Please put it on.

01:11:32.400 --> 01:11:35.740
What's the matter with you? You come to us every day.

01:11:35.860 --> 01:11:37.520
What are you doing in there?

01:11:38.310 --> 01:11:40.660
Answer me!

01:11:41.090 --> 01:11:44.430
Everyone can nod. You should talk.

01:11:44.580 --> 01:11:48.090
Can't you speak German? You could have learned it long ago.

01:11:48.210 --> 01:11:50.880
You've been here long enough. Now you're leaving.

01:11:51.170 --> 01:11:59.000
Why are you staring at me like that? Have you never seen a Turk before?

01:12:07.480 --> 01:12:11.360
- Where are you taking me? - International Police Headquarters. Just a check-up.

01:12:19.440 --> 01:12:22.850
I'm sorry, miss. It's orders. We can't go against the protocol.

01:12:23.000 --> 01:12:26.130
- I don't even know what protocol means. - Neither do I, miss.

01:12:26.790 --> 01:12:30.000
- What's that? - Your lipstick.

01:12:44.750 --> 01:12:45.870
All right, all right, keep out of this.

01:12:45.910 --> 01:12:47.030
Listen, I've got to talk to you.

01:12:47.340 --> 01:12:49.420
I've seen a dead man walking.

01:12:49.460 --> 01:12:50.340
All right, son, get back.

01:12:50.680 --> 01:12:51.510
I saw him buried.

01:12:51.850 --> 01:12:53.910
And now I've seen him alive.

01:13:02.490 --> 01:13:04.770
Just a minute. Bring her in here.

01:13:04.840 --> 01:13:12.580
Stay out of here. Come in, Mishmit.

01:13:12.810 --> 01:13:16.320
Now then, Mishmit.

01:13:16.360 --> 01:13:19.360
I'm not interested in your forged papers. That's purely a Russian case.

01:13:19.550 --> 01:13:23.000
- When did you last see live? - Two weeks ago.

01:13:23.100 --> 01:13:25.100
I want the truth, Mishmit. We know he's alive.

01:13:25.120 --> 01:13:27.910
It is true then.

01:13:28.160 --> 01:13:30.000
Joseph Harbin's body was found in the cottage.

01:13:32.520 --> 01:13:40.840
you say? I'm sorry. I said another man was buried in his place. Where's Harry? That's what we want

01:13:40.840 --> 01:13:51.400
to find out. I'm sorry. I don't seem able to understand anything you say. He's alive. Now this

01:13:51.400 --> 01:13:56.470
minute he's doing something. Miss Schmidt, we know he's somewhere across the canal in the Russian

01:13:56.470 --> 01:14:00.000
sector. You may as well help us. In a few minutes, Colonel Brotson.

01:14:00.060 --> 01:14:01.620
He will be questioning you about your papers.

01:14:02.020 --> 01:14:02.840
Tell me where Lime is.

01:14:03.470 --> 01:14:04.510
I don't know.

01:14:05.530 --> 01:14:07.350
If you help me, I am prepared to help you.

01:14:09.300 --> 01:14:10.980
Martin's always said you were a fool.

01:14:12.570 --> 01:14:14.070
Vienna is a closed city, Miss Schmidt.

01:14:14.130 --> 01:14:14.890
He can't get away.

01:14:15.290 --> 01:14:15.550
Right.

01:14:16.700 --> 01:14:17.400
Poor Harry.

01:14:18.690 --> 01:14:20.630
I wish he was dead.

01:14:22.860 --> 01:14:24.480
He would be safe from all of you then.

01:14:35.240 --> 01:14:38.230
Why, that's you. Come up.

01:14:39.220 --> 01:14:42.510
I want to speak to you, Kurtz.

01:14:42.610 --> 01:14:43.980
Of course. Come up.

01:14:44.700 --> 01:14:45.700
I'll wait here.

01:14:46.120 --> 01:14:47.030
I don't understand.

01:14:47.510 --> 01:14:49.110
I want to talk to Harry.

01:14:49.770 --> 01:14:51.260
Are you mad?

01:14:51.530 --> 01:14:53.730
All right, I'm mad. I've seen a ghost.

01:14:54.280 --> 01:14:56.120
You tell Harry I want to see it.

01:14:57.790 --> 01:14:59.800
Be reasonable. Come up and talk.

01:15:00.160 --> 01:15:02.880
No, thank you. I like the ocean.

01:15:03.660 --> 01:15:06.120
Tell him I'll wait by that wheel there.

01:15:06.310 --> 01:15:10.530
Or the ghosts only rise by night, Dr. Finkle.

01:15:10.560 --> 01:15:13.670
You got an opinion on that?

01:16:22.010 --> 01:16:23.970
seem to be giving you quite some busy time.

01:16:23.970 --> 01:16:26.150
Listen, I want to talk to you.

01:16:26.330 --> 01:16:27.810
Talk to me? Of course.

01:16:28.780 --> 01:16:29.350
Come on.

01:16:33.070 --> 01:16:36.610
Kids used to ride this thing a lot in the old days, but they have the money now for it ever.

01:16:36.610 --> 01:16:38.240
- It's my sterk. - It's the gate in Ogden.

01:16:38.240 --> 01:16:40.980
- Oh, vielen Dank. - Listen, Harry, I didn't believe it.

01:16:40.980 --> 01:16:47.200
- Good to see you, Holly. Was that your funeral? - It was pretty smart, wasn't it?

01:16:47.200 --> 01:16:51.700
Oh, the same old indigestion, Holly. These are the only things that help, these tablets.

01:16:51.700 --> 01:16:54.800
These are the last. Can't get them anywhere in Europe anymore.

01:16:54.800 --> 01:16:57.850
You know what's happened to your girl? She's been arrested.

01:16:57.850 --> 01:17:00.000
Tough, very tough, but don't worry all the time.

01:17:00.000 --> 01:17:02.950
- They won't hurt her. - You're handing her over to the Russians.

01:17:02.950 --> 01:17:05.950
- What can I do, old man? I'm dead on her. - You can help somehow.

01:17:05.950 --> 01:17:08.910
- Holly. - Exactly.

01:17:08.910 --> 01:17:12.030
- Who did you tell about me? - I told the police.

01:17:12.030 --> 01:17:14.030
- Unwise, Holly. - And Anna?

01:17:14.030 --> 01:17:17.800
- Unwise. - Did the police believe you?

01:17:17.800 --> 01:17:22.130
- You don't care anything at all about Anna, do you? - I've got quite a lot on my mind.

01:17:22.130 --> 01:17:25.140
- You wouldn't do anything. - What do you want me to do? You'll reason it.

01:17:25.140 --> 01:17:27.140
- Somebody else didn't expect me to give myself up. - Why not?

01:17:27.140 --> 01:17:30.000
It's a far better thing than I do with the old limelight.

01:17:30.570 --> 01:17:32.490
Follow the curtain up.

01:17:32.490 --> 01:17:33.790
Holly, you and I aren't heroes.

01:17:33.790 --> 01:17:35.290
The world doesn't make any heroes.

01:17:35.290 --> 01:17:37.210
- You've got plenty of contacts. - Outside of your stories.

01:17:37.210 --> 01:17:39.470
I've got to be so careful.

01:17:39.470 --> 01:17:41.010
I'm only safe in the Russian zone.

01:17:41.010 --> 01:17:43.350
I'm only safe here, as long as they can use me.

01:17:43.350 --> 01:17:44.570
As long as they can use you.

01:17:44.570 --> 01:17:46.090
I wish I could get rid of this thing.

01:17:46.090 --> 01:17:48.100
So that's how they found out about Anna.

01:17:48.100 --> 01:17:50.150
You told them, didn't you?

01:17:50.150 --> 01:17:52.110
Don't try to be a policeman, old man.

01:17:52.110 --> 01:17:53.230
What do you expect me to be?

01:17:53.230 --> 01:17:54.070
Part of your--

01:17:54.070 --> 01:17:56.190
You can have any parts you want, as long as you don't interfere.

01:17:56.190 --> 01:17:58.510
I've never cut you out of anything.

01:17:58.510 --> 01:18:00.000
I remember when they raided that gambling

01:18:00.120 --> 01:18:01.320
and you're a safe way out.

01:18:01.320 --> 01:18:04.120
- Sure. - Yes, safe for you, not safe for me.

01:18:04.120 --> 01:18:08.000
Old man, you never should have gone to the police, you know.

01:18:08.000 --> 01:18:11.750
You ought to leave this thing alone.

01:18:11.750 --> 01:18:14.730
Have you ever seen any of your victims?

01:18:14.730 --> 01:18:18.190
You know, I never feel comfortable in these sort of things.

01:18:18.190 --> 01:18:21.820
- Victims? - Be melodramatic.

01:18:21.820 --> 01:18:24.740
Look down there.

01:18:24.740 --> 01:18:30.000
Would you really feel any pity if one of those dots stopped moving forever?

01:18:31.210 --> 01:18:36.350
If I offered you 20,000 pounds for every dot that stopped, would you really, old man, tell me to keep my money?

01:18:36.490 --> 01:18:40.550
Or would you calculate how many dots you could afford to spare?

01:18:40.770 --> 01:18:41.950
Free of income tax, only.

01:18:42.320 --> 01:18:43.320
Free of income tax?

01:18:44.290 --> 01:18:45.850
Another way you can save money now, dear.

01:18:46.580 --> 01:18:48.100
A lot of good your money will do you in jail.

01:18:48.160 --> 01:18:49.900
That jail's in another zone.

01:18:51.100 --> 01:18:54.640
There's no proof against me, besides you.

01:19:03.550 --> 01:19:09.930
- I should be pretty easy to get rid of. - Pretty easy?

01:19:09.930 --> 01:19:13.960
- Too sure. - I carry a gun.

01:19:13.960 --> 01:19:18.830
Don't think they'd look for a bullet wound after you hit that ground.

01:19:18.830 --> 01:19:26.880
- Dug up your coffin. - Found Harvard?

01:19:32.360 --> 01:19:44.460
- Get in. - Holly, what fools we are talking to each other this way as though I'd do anything to you, or you to me.

01:19:44.460 --> 01:19:50.740
You're just a little mixed up about things in general. Nobody thinks in terms of human beings.

01:19:50.740 --> 01:19:54.690
Governments don't. Why should we? They talk about the people and the proletariat.

01:19:54.690 --> 01:20:00.000
I talk about the suckers and the mugs. It's the same thing. They have their...

01:20:00.060 --> 01:20:01.200
Five-year plans.

01:20:02.140 --> 01:20:02.960
So have I.

01:20:03.310 --> 01:20:04.510
I used to believe in God.

01:20:06.110 --> 01:20:07.970
I still do believe in God only.

01:20:09.410 --> 01:20:13.470
I believe in God and mercy and all that.

01:20:13.570 --> 01:20:15.530
But dead are happier dead.

01:20:16.090 --> 01:20:18.630
I don't miss much here, poor devils.

01:20:20.260 --> 01:20:23.950
What do you believe in?

01:20:25.220 --> 01:20:28.200
Oh, if you ever get Anna out of this mess, be kind to her.

01:20:28.580 --> 01:20:30.000
You'll find she's worth it.

01:20:30.540 --> 01:20:33.300
I wish I'd asked you to bring me some of these tablets from home.

01:20:33.300 --> 01:20:36.400
Holly, I'd like to cut you in, old man.

01:20:36.400 --> 01:20:39.050
There's nobody left in Vienna. I can really trust.

01:20:39.050 --> 01:20:41.050
And we've always done everything together.

01:20:41.050 --> 01:20:43.010
When you make up your mind, send me a message.

01:20:43.010 --> 01:20:45.010
I'll meet you any place, any time.

01:20:45.010 --> 01:20:49.430
And when we do meet, old man, it's you I want to see, not the police.

01:20:49.430 --> 01:20:51.270
Remember that, won't you?

01:20:51.270 --> 01:20:55.640
Don't be so gloomy. After all, it's not that awful.

01:20:55.640 --> 01:21:00.000
But what the fella said, Natalie, for 30 years, onto the border.

01:21:00.000 --> 01:21:03.000
In Borgias, they had warfare, terror, murder, and bloodshed,

01:21:03.000 --> 01:21:07.020
but they produced Michelangelo, Leonardo da Vinci, and the Renaissance.

01:21:07.020 --> 01:21:09.020
In Switzerland, they had brotherly love.

01:21:09.020 --> 01:21:12.140
They had 500 years of democracy and peace.

01:21:12.140 --> 01:21:13.140
And what did that produce?

01:21:13.140 --> 01:21:15.470
The cuckoo clock.

01:21:15.470 --> 01:21:17.320
So long, Colin.

01:21:17.320 --> 01:21:29.460
Here, Martin's, you can always arrange to meet him at some little cafe here.

01:21:30.000 --> 01:21:32.000
- Wouldn't work. - We'll never get him in the Russian zone.

01:21:32.000 --> 01:21:34.000
Callaway, you expect too much.

01:21:34.000 --> 01:21:36.790
Oh, I know he deserves to hang.

01:21:36.790 --> 01:21:40.670
You've proved your stuff, but 20 years is a long time.

01:21:40.670 --> 01:21:43.360
Don't ask me to tie the rope.

01:21:43.360 --> 01:21:45.820
Okay, forget it.

01:21:45.820 --> 01:21:49.860
- Busy, Major? - What is it, Blonsky?

01:21:49.860 --> 01:21:53.080
We have identified the girl. Here is her report.

01:21:53.080 --> 01:21:56.860
I've questioned her. We've got nothing against her.

01:21:56.860 --> 01:22:00.000
We shall apply for her at the 4-hour meeting.

01:22:01.370 --> 01:22:07.370
- She has no right to be here. - I've asked your people to help with Lyme.

01:22:07.850 --> 01:22:11.130
That's a different case. It is being looked into.

01:22:11.800 --> 01:22:12.860
So long, Major.

01:22:14.630 --> 01:22:18.630
In the last war, a general would hang his opponent's picture on the wall.

01:22:20.200 --> 01:22:21.500
He got to know him that way.

01:22:22.230 --> 01:22:23.930
I'm beginning to know Lyme.

01:22:25.540 --> 01:22:28.840
I think this would have worked with your help.

01:22:31.340 --> 01:22:37.460
What price would you pay?

01:23:04.760 --> 01:23:11.590
Marcia, Sanfay, Stellanti, Innsbruck, Landeck, Benzir.

01:23:11.590 --> 01:23:17.120
Here we are. You'll be all right here, miss.

01:23:17.120 --> 01:23:23.470
I don't understand, Major Calloway.

01:23:23.470 --> 01:23:25.430
I expect he's got a soft spot for you, miss.

01:23:25.430 --> 01:23:27.330
Why has he done all this?

01:23:27.330 --> 01:23:30.000
Don't you worry, miss. You're welcome.

01:23:30.000 --> 01:23:40.280
out the things. There we are miss. Thank you. You've been so kind. Well I'll be saying good night. Good night.

01:24:19.640 --> 01:24:23.840
Are you going to?

01:24:23.840 --> 01:24:26.980
See you off.

01:24:26.980 --> 01:24:28.310
See me off?

01:24:28.310 --> 01:24:29.330
From here?

01:24:29.330 --> 01:24:30.000
Oh, I watch you.

01:24:30.000 --> 01:24:32.340
No harm in that, is there?

01:24:32.340 --> 01:24:34.900
How did you know I'd be here?

01:24:34.900 --> 01:24:38.350
Why, I heard something about it at police headquarters.

01:24:38.350 --> 01:24:41.870
Have you been seeing Major Callaway again?

01:24:41.870 --> 01:24:44.980
Of course not. I don't live in his pocket.

01:24:44.980 --> 01:24:47.440
Harry, what is it?

01:24:47.440 --> 01:24:52.050
For heaven's sake, stop calling me Harry.

01:24:52.050 --> 01:24:54.030
I'm sorry.

01:24:54.030 --> 01:24:56.380
What's in your mind? Where did you hide?

01:24:56.380 --> 01:24:59.380
Hide? Can I have a drink?

01:25:00.320 --> 01:25:02.280
- It's cold on that train. - I shall be all right.

01:25:02.360 --> 01:25:05.280
- You send me a wire as soon as you are. - What's going to happen? Where's Harry?

01:25:05.360 --> 01:25:07.750
- He's safe in the Russian zone. - How do you know?

01:25:07.780 --> 01:25:09.570
- I saw him today. - How is he?

01:25:09.650 --> 01:25:11.450
He can look after himself. Don't worry.

01:25:11.530 --> 01:25:14.450
- Did he say anything about me? Tell me. - The usual things.

01:25:14.530 --> 01:25:16.870
There's something wrong. Did you tell Calloway about meeting Harry?

01:25:16.950 --> 01:25:18.950
- Of course I didn't tell Calloway. - Why should he help me like that?

01:25:19.030 --> 01:25:21.250
- The Russians will only make trouble for you. - That's his headache.

01:25:21.330 --> 01:25:24.910
- His? Why are you lying? - We're getting you out of here, aren't we?

01:25:24.990 --> 01:25:30.000
- I'm not going. - Anna, don't you recognize

01:25:30.060 --> 01:25:31.660
a good turn when you see one.

01:25:31.660 --> 01:25:34.210
You have seen Calloway. What are you two doing?

01:25:34.210 --> 01:25:39.750
Well, they asked me to help take him.

01:25:39.750 --> 01:25:42.160
I'm helping.

01:25:42.160 --> 01:26:00.000
Couldn't even lift up.

01:26:00.090 --> 01:26:04.110
Oh, you've got your precious honesty and don't want anything else.

01:26:04.110 --> 01:26:06.330
You still want him.

01:26:06.330 --> 01:26:08.370
I don't want him anymore.

01:26:08.370 --> 01:26:10.710
I don't want to see him, hear him.

01:26:10.710 --> 01:26:12.530
But he's still a part of me.

01:26:12.530 --> 01:26:13.530
That's a fact.

01:26:13.530 --> 01:26:20.350
I couldn't do a thing to harm him.

01:26:36.780 --> 01:26:44.930
Anna, why do we always have to quarrel? If you want to sell your services, I'm not willing to be the

01:26:44.930 --> 01:27:00.000
price. I loved him. You loved him. What good have we done him? Love. Look at yourself. They have a name for

01:27:00.120 --> 01:27:26.530
- There isn't much time. - I want to get a plane out of here tonight.

01:27:26.530 --> 01:27:30.000
So she talked about it.

01:27:30.000 --> 01:27:31.800
- Get you out of it. - Gave me these.

01:27:32.170 --> 01:27:38.410
- A girl of spirit. - She's right. It is none of my business.

01:27:38.480 --> 01:27:42.750
It won't make any difference in the long run. I'll get him.

01:27:42.800 --> 01:27:47.030
- Well, I won't have helped. - That'll be a fine best to make.

01:27:47.030 --> 01:27:50.450
Well, I always wanted you to catch that plane, didn't I?

01:27:50.460 --> 01:27:54.130
- You all did. - I'd better see if there's anyone still at the terminus.

01:27:54.210 --> 01:28:00.000
You may need a priority. Mind if I drop off somewhere on the way?

01:28:00.250 --> 01:28:02.250
I've got an appointment. It won't take five minutes.

01:28:02.250 --> 01:28:03.420
Of course.

01:28:03.420 --> 01:28:05.920
Why don't you come in too? You're a writer. It might interest you.

01:28:05.920 --> 01:28:10.270
This is the biggest children's hospital in Vienna.

01:28:10.270 --> 01:28:13.120
All the kids in here are the result of Lyme's penicillin racket.

01:28:34.510 --> 01:28:35.390
It had meningitis.

01:28:36.530 --> 01:28:38.230
They gave it some of the limes, penicillin.

01:28:39.480 --> 01:28:41.110
Terrible pity, isn't it?

01:29:08.760 --> 01:29:10.160
Payne lent me one of your books.

01:29:10.710 --> 01:29:12.290
Oklahoma Kid, I think it was.

01:29:12.750 --> 01:29:13.530
I read a bit of it.

01:29:13.590 --> 01:29:14.730
It looked as if it was going to be pretty good.

01:29:15.410 --> 01:29:17.370
What made you take up this sort of thing?

01:29:17.950 --> 01:29:19.440
Been doing it for long?

01:29:20.090 --> 01:29:21.390
All right, Callaway, you win.

01:29:21.410 --> 01:29:23.230
I never knew there was Snake Chalmers in Texas.

01:29:23.310 --> 01:29:24.430
I said you win.

01:29:24.710 --> 01:29:25.210
Win what?

01:29:26.210 --> 01:29:30.000
I'll be your dumb decoy duck.

01:31:01.120 --> 01:31:13.740
- Look, sir. - How much longer are you going to sit here?

01:31:13.820 --> 01:31:16.960
- Shall I go over there, sir? - No.

01:31:41.550 --> 01:31:49.070
- No, thank you. No. - Keep going, don't you understand?

01:31:49.180 --> 01:31:59.830
- Go on, my lord. - Go on, I beg you.

01:32:00.890 --> 01:32:02.300
Come on. Snell. Snell.

01:32:02.590 --> 01:32:04.260
All right. All right. Only one.

01:32:04.680 --> 01:32:05.860
Corn's copper.

01:32:44.520 --> 01:32:48.340
- How did you know I was here, anyway? - From courts.

01:32:48.420 --> 01:32:54.070
They've just been arrested. But Harry won't come. He's not a fool.

01:32:54.150 --> 01:32:57.400
Yes, Payne. Slip over there. See what she's up to.

01:32:57.480 --> 01:33:00.000
- Right, sir. - Don't tell me you're doing all this.

01:33:00.000 --> 01:33:05.410
- What's your price this time? - No price, Anna.

01:33:05.410 --> 01:33:09.510
Honest, sensible, sober, harmless Holly Martins.

01:33:09.510 --> 01:33:14.080
Holly, what a silly name.

01:33:14.080 --> 01:33:17.640
You must feel very proud to be a police informer.

01:33:17.640 --> 01:33:23.410
Harry, get away! The police are outside! Quick!

01:33:23.410 --> 01:33:25.760
Anna!

01:33:32.110 --> 01:33:32.660
Shit!

01:35:00.510 --> 01:35:03.970
We're at the Danube Canal. The wind is blowing.

01:35:04.090 --> 01:35:14.470
The wind is blowing. We're off.

01:36:04.830 --> 01:36:07.140
on the way to Stuttgart.

01:36:30.640 --> 01:36:33.400
- I don't want to. - Okay.

01:36:34.490 --> 01:36:38.770
Let's get out of here and get out of here.

01:37:03.550 --> 01:37:07.270
Not you! There they are! Go!

01:38:34.410 --> 01:38:36.920
- The count, please. - Sir.

01:38:36.920 --> 01:38:55.320
- Please, if we can shoot a-- - Sir, please, I think he got it.

01:39:05.280 --> 01:39:09.920
that you? You're through, Harry. Come out. You haven't got a chance this way. What do

01:39:09.920 --> 01:39:18.920
you want? You might as well give up. Give back! Give back! Hey, come back, sir!

01:41:37.390 --> 01:41:40.280
Lord, give them eternal rest.

01:41:40.330 --> 01:41:43.540
Let them rest in peace. Amen.

01:42:04.190 --> 01:42:08.520
- What time is it? - 2:30.

01:42:08.520 --> 01:42:14.920
I'll have to step on it if you're going to catch that plane.

01:42:14.920 --> 01:42:19.350
Callaway, can't you do something about Anna?

01:42:19.350 --> 01:42:22.440
I'll do what I can, if she'll let me.

01:42:31.500 --> 01:42:34.260
Wait a minute. Let me out.

01:42:34.260 --> 01:42:36.160
Well, there's not much time.

01:42:36.160 --> 01:42:40.560
One can't just leave, please.

01:42:40.560 --> 01:42:43.600
Be sensible, Martins.

01:42:43.600 --> 01:42:46.870
Haven't got a sensible name, Calloway.

01:43:30.570 --> 01:43:31.250
Oh
